<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>CFBundleDevelopmentRegion</key>
	<string>en</string>
	<key>CFBundleDisplayName</key>
	<string>Lingua</string>
	<key>CFBundleExecutable</key>
	<string>$(EXECUTABLE_NAME)</string>
	<key>CFBundleIdentifier</key>
	<string>$(PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER)</string>
	<key>CFBundleInfoDictionaryVersion</key>
	<string>6.0</string>
	<key>CFBundleName</key>
	<string>$(PRODUCT_NAME)</string>
	<key>CFBundlePackageType</key>
	<string>XPC!</string>
	<key>CFBundleShortVersionString</key>
	<string>$(MARKETING_VERSION)</string>
	<key>CFBundleVersion</key>
	<string>$(CURRENT_PROJECT_VERSION)</string>
	<key>NSExtension</key>
	<dict>
		<key>NSExtensionAttributes</key>
		<dict>
			<key>IsASCIICapable</key>
			<false/>
			<key>PrefersRightToLeft</key>
			<false/>
			<key>PrimaryLanguage</key>
			<string>mul</string>
			<!-- NOTHING THIS KEYBOARD DOES NEEDS THIS, and it is still true.
			     It is left true on purpose, and only the owner takes it out.

			     What open access grants, and what this extension does with
			     each of them — measured across the six Swift files here, not
			     assumed:

			       write to the App Group   Shared.board() only READS. The
			                                writing is the app's, in
			                                ios/App/App/LinguaShare.swift.
			       the network              no URLSession, no URLRequest, no
			                                http anywhere under LinguaKeyboard.
			       microphone and speaker   no AVAudio, no AudioServices, no
			                                playInputClick.
			       Location and Contacts    no CLLocation, no CNContact.
			       iCloud, Game Center,     no NSUbiquitous, no StoreKit,
			       In-App Purchase          no SKPayment.

			     AND READING THE APP GROUP DOES NOT NEED IT. Apple's own
			     current page, "Configuring open access for a custom
			     keyboard", says the default sandbox "prevents writing to the
			     containing app's shared group containers (reading is
			     permitted)", and lists among the capabilities of a keyboard
			     WITHOUT open access: "read-only access to the containing
			     app's shared containers". The iOS 8 App Extension Programming
			     Guide's "cannot share a container with its containing app" is
			     the archived sentence this was built on, and it is superseded.
			     KeyboardViewController.build() carries the same quotation
			     over the full-access gate that was taken out of it.

			     So the paper answer is that this can be false, and App Store
			     Review 4.4.1 pushes that way — a keyboard must remain
			     functional without requiring full access.

			     IT IS NOT TAKEN OUT HERE, AND THE REASON IS THE COST OF BEING
			     WRONG. There is no Swift on a Linux runner and no check in
			     this repository can open a keyboard, so every sentence above
			     is read rather than pressed. If reading the group turns out
			     to need the switch after all, this keyboard draws NOT ONE KEY
			     and the person has no way back — they cannot turn on a switch
			     the app no longer asks for.

			     IT IS ANSWERED BY ONE PRESS ON THE BUILD THAT IS ALREADY OUT,
			     and no new build: turn Allow Full Access OFF for Lingua in
			     iOS Settings, then open the keyboard. Drawn letters still
			     there means false is safe. docs/apple.md § 2c. -->
			<key>RequestsOpenAccess</key>
			<true/>
		</dict>
		<key>NSExtensionPointIdentifier</key>
		<string>com.apple.keyboard-service</string>
		<key>NSExtensionPrincipalClass</key>
		<string>$(PRODUCT_MODULE_NAME).KeyboardViewController</string>
	</dict>
</dict>
</plist>
